FASE 5 answered "which clients does Boop manage?". This one answers "what work
is being done, and where is it?".

## The portal had a fake identity, and no authorization

`DEMO_PROJECT_ID` was `hartmann.PROJECT.id` and it decided WHO SAW WHAT:
`/portal` redirected to it, `assertProject()` compared against it, and
`/bem-vindo` showed the same client to anyone who logged in. It was not a data
mock — it was an identity mock. And `assertProject()` compared a string without
asking the database, so the portal had no real authorization at all.

`/portal` is now a RESOLVER: it asks which projects the actor reaches (RLS) and
which should appear to them (D-18), then decides — none shows an empty state
with a voice, one redirects, several offer a choice. `.first()` is never picked.

The guard lives in the LAYOUT of `/portal/[projectId]`, not in each page: a
per-page guard is a guard the next page forgets. Every loader repeats it anyway,
because a loader that is safe only because its caller is safe stops being safe
in the second caller.

## RLS answers tenant; the projection answers visibility

`projects_select` grants a draft's row to its own client, and that is right —
Boop needs drafts to work on them, and `authenticated` is one role for all three
personas. So the product rule lives in the server, pure and testable, exactly
like the `clients.notes` projection: a draft never reaches a `client_user`, not
even by direct URL.

## The journey is the progress

No percentage anywhere. One source of truth per question — order is `position`,
"where we are" is `state = 'current'`, and there is no "first not done"
heuristic. Zero current stages is legitimate when the journey ended; when it is
not, `advanceStage` refuses instead of guessing, and `setStageState` fixes it.

`social.v1` has the EIGHT stages from docs/product.md, not the six from the
prototype mock: the mock was screen illustration, the document is the spec. The
other four types get minimal journeys — proof the architecture does not depend
on social.

## D-16: scope is gone, team is real

`scope` had no origin in the schema, so the block left the screen instead of
becoming an invented column. `team` stayed and became real: Boop people with an
EXPLICIT membership. A `boop_admin` reaches every client by D-08 and that does
not put them on anyone's team — access is not allocation. No job titles: the V0
does not store one, and deriving "Estrategista" from `boop_member` would be
fiction on the client's screen.

Also: `project.change_status` existed in the permission matrix since FASE 0 and
was missing from `CAPABILITIES`. No hole — `projects_update` already required
`is_boop()` and a membership — but the documented row had no counterpart in
`can()`. `setStageState` deliberately does not get its own capability.

